nokio","title":"Pinokio","items":[{"url":"https://pinokio.co/apps/github-com-pinokiofactory-e2-f5-tts/checkpoints/sha256%3Adf082bc4e4fafcddf59a58ec8a08467df10f8a00cdc44645a22a87a80ff7f54b","domain":"pinokio.co","title":"Pinokio","pub_date":1771027200}]}]},{"key":"https://pinokio.co/apps/github-com-pinokiofactory-zonos/checkpoints/sha256%3A02d61d6c4746aefeda9f1108867b62f3fb75afb5c9d8302aec3c046810f5aa36","domain":"pinokio.co","url":"https://pinokio.co/apps/github-com-pinokiofactory-zonos/checkpoints/sha256%3A02d61d6c4746aefeda9f1108867b62f3fb75afb5c9d8302aec3c046810f5aa36","titles":[{"key":"https://pinokio.co/apps/github-com-pinokiofactory-zonos/checkpoints/sha256%3A02d61d6c4746aefeda9f1108867b62f3fb75afb5c9d8302aec3c046810f5aa36-Pinokio","title":"Pinokio","items":[{"url":"https://pinokio.co/apps/github-com-pinokiofactory-zonos/checkpoints/sha256%3A02d61d6c4746aefeda9f1108867b62f3fb75afb5c9d8302aec3c046810f5aa36","domain":"pinokio.co","title":"Pinokio","pub_date":1782864000}]}]},{"key":"https://beta.pinokio.co/apps/github-com-pinokiocomputer-plugin","domain":"beta.pinokio.co","url":"https://beta.pinokio.co/apps/github-com-pinokiocomputer-plugin","titles":[{"key":"https://beta.pinokio.co/apps/github-com-pinokiocomputer-plugin-plugin on Pinokio","title":"plugin on Pinokio","items":[{"url":"https://beta.pinokio.co/apps/github-com-pinokiocomputer-plugin","domain":"beta.pinokio.co","title":"plugin on Pinokio"}]}]},{"key":"https://jan.ai/developer/framework/engineering/fine-tuning/","domain":"jan.ai","url":"https://jan.ai/developer/framework/engineering/fine-tuning/","titles":[{"key":"https://jan.ai/developer/framework/engineering/fine-tuning/-Jan - Jan","title":"Jan - Jan","items":[{"url":"https://jan.ai/developer/framework/engineering/fine-tuning/","domain":"jan.ai","title":"Jan - Jan"}]}]},{"key":"https://www.techradar.com/pro/security/anthropics-official-git-mcp-server-had-some-worrying-security-flaws-this-is-what-happened-next","domain":"techradar.com","url":"https://www.techradar.com/pro/security/anthropics-official-git-mcp-server-had-some-worrying-security-flaws-this-is-what-happened-next","titles":[{"key":"https://www.techradar.com/pro/security/anthropics-official-git-mcp-server-had-some-worrying-security-flaws-this-is-what-happened-next-Anthropic's official Git MCP server had some worrying security flaws - this is what happened next","title":"Anthropic's official Git MCP server had some worrying security flaws - this is what happened next","items":[{"url":"https://www.techradar.com/pro/security/anthropics-official-git-mcp-server-had-some-worrying-security-flaws-this-is-what-happened-next","domain":"techradar.com","title":"Anthropic's official Git MCP server had some worrying security flaws - this is what happened next","snippet":"In January 2026, TechRadar reported that Anthropic had addressed several critical security vulnerabilities in its Git Model Context Protocol (MCP) server, which powers AI interactions with Git repositories. Discovered by the security startup Cyata, the flaws—namely a path validation bypass (CVE-2025-68145), an unrestricted git_init issue (CVE-2025-68143), and argument injection in git_diff (CVE-2025-68144)—could be chained with other MCP tools like the Filesystem MCP server to enable remote code execution or file tampering through prompt injection.\n\nThe vulnerabilities were reported in June 2025 and patched by December 2025 in version 2025.12.18. There is no current evidence of these bugs being exploited. The incident highlights the risks posed by complex \"agentic\" AI systems where individual safe components can become dangerous in combination. This comes amid broader concerns about AI misuse, with Anthropic previously revealing that its Claude Code tool was manipulated in a cyber espionage campaign targeting major tech companies, governments, and financial institutions.","pub_date":1769016600}]}]},{"key":"https://www.tomshardware.com/tech-industry/artificial-intelligence/anthropics-model-context-protocol-has-critical-security-flaw-exposed","domain":"tomshardware.com","url":"https://www.tomshardware.com/tech-industry/artificial-intelligence/anthropics-model-context-protocol-has-critical-security-flaw-exposed","titles":[{"key":"https://www.tomshardware.com/tech-industry/artificial-intelligence/anthropics-model-context-protocol-has-critical-security-flaw-exposed-Anthropic's Model Context Protocol includes a critical remote code execution vulnerability - newly discovered exploit puts 200,000 AI servers at risk","title":"Anthropic's Model Context Protocol includes a critical remote code execution vulnerability - newly discovered exploit puts 200,000 AI servers at risk","items":[{"url":"https://www.tomshardware.com/tech-industry/artificial-intelligence/anthropics-model-context-protocol-has-critical-security-flaw-exposed","domain":"tomshardware.com","title":"Anthropic's Model Context Protocol includes a critical remote code execution vulnerability - newly discovered exploit puts 200,000 AI servers at risk","snippet":"Security researchers at OX Security have uncovered a critical remote code execution vulnerability in Anthropic's Model Context Protocol (MCP), threatening as many as 200,000 AI servers. MCP, introduced in 2024 to enable AI models to interface with external tools and widely adopted by major tech firms, suffers from unsafe STDIO-based input handling in its SDKs (Python, TypeScript, Java, and Rust). This flaw lets user-controlled inputs lead directly to unsanitized command execution.\n\nDespite the severity—impacting over 150 million SDK downloads and multiple live production systems—Anthropic declined to patch the protocol, calling the behavior \"expected.\" OX Security demonstrated serious exploits including UI injection, tool hardening bypasses, zero-click prompt injection in IDEs, and malicious package uploads to MCP marketplaces, successfully achieving command execution across multiple platforms.\n\nTen high/critical CVEs have been logged, but fixes have been limited. Anthropic continues to maintain the vulnerable SDKs even after the protocol's donation to the Linux Foundation. Ironically, this comes shortly after the company promoted its Claude Mythos model for identifying software vulnerabilities. The situation underscores the urgent need for protocol-level security reforms and input sanitization by all downstream developers.","pub_date":1776855600}]}]},{"key":"https://github.com/janhq","domain":"github.com","url":"https://github.com/janhq","titles":[{"key":"https://github.com/janhq-Jan · GitHub","title":"Jan · GitHub","items":[{"url":"https://github.com/janhq","domain":"github.com","title":"Jan · GitHub","snippet":"Image: @janhq  JAN  Jan is building Open Intelligence.  * Verified  We've verified that the organization janhq controls the domains:  * jan.ai * menlo.ai  Learn more about verified organizations  *..."}]}]},{"key":"https://github.com/janhq/jan","domain":"github.com","url":"https://github.com/janhq/jan","titles":[{"key":"https://github.com/janhq/jan-GitHub - janhq/jan: Jan is an open source alternative to ChatGPT that runs 100% offline on your computer. · GitHub","title":"GitHub - janhq/jan: Jan is an open source alternative to ChatGPT that runs 100% offline on your computer. · GitHub","items":[{"url":"https://github.com/janhq/jan","domain":"github.com","title":"GitHub - janhq/jan: Jan is an open source alternative to ChatGPT that runs 100% offline on your computer. · GitHub"}]}]},{"key":"https://github.com/open-webui","domain":"github.com","url":"https://github.com/open-webui","titles":[{"key":"https://github.com/open-webui-Open WebUI · GitHub","title":"Open WebUI · GitHub","items":[{"url":"https://github.com/open-webui","domain":"github.com","title":"Open WebUI · GitHub","snippet":"Image: @open-webui  OPEN WEBUI  On a mission to build the best AI user interface.  *   * 5.4k followers * https://openwebui.com * @OpenWebUI * support@openwebui.com  README.md  A HOME FOR AI.  Create..."}]}]},{"key"